y protective  all this visual politic fans is much  easier said than done how could ukraine  make a u-turn will the oligarchs allow  it and remember we're talking about a  country that has hundreds and hundreds  and thousands millions of young  immigrants all over europe young people  with professional skills and in many  cases good academic training young  people who could play a key role in the  process of economic and institutional  reconstruction that the country needs to  carry out before it can accede to the  european union in fact let's be clear  without attracting the diaspora back it  will be very difficult for ukraine to  achieve the level of prosperity wealth  and development necessary to complete  its accession to the european union in a  reasonable period of time but of course  in order for them to be able to return  invest or set up companies two things  are needed above all legal certainty and  open markets the question is how could  these two things be achieved  well some say that one way to achieve  this would be to emulate the model of  dubai's special economic zones as in the  case of the dubai international  financial center there british judges  train other judges and impart justice  following a special legal framework a  framework of law that is very protective  and very attractive to foreign companies  and investors now is this realistic and  is there even a remote possibility that  it will even be possible well probably  not but cases like that of oligarch  rinat akhmetov paradoxically do give  some hope akhmetov for those of you who  don't know him is the richest man in  ukraine and a businessman who always had  close connections to russia and the  pro-russian mileage of ukrainian  politics in fact he has been the main  financier and shadow leader of the  russophile party of regions with which  he became a lawmaker in the virk  hovnerada well pay attention to this  statement  evil cannot go unpunished russia's  crimes against ukraine and our people  are egregious and those guilty of them  must be held liable this lawsuit is one  of the first international legal steps  against russia to stop their ongoing  crimes destruction of the ukrainian  economy and the plundering of ukrainian  assets renat akhmanitov ukrainian  oligarch whether for money for a future  in ukraine or for a sudden love of his  homeland or for all of these things at  once the rhetoric of akhmetov owner  among many other things of the famous  azov still steel works has changed  radically  [Music]  well like him many other pro-russian  oligarchs are suffering multi-million  dollar losses due to the russian  invasion without going any further  akmetov's wealth has shrunk from its  peak of 16 billion dollars in 2012 to  about 4.2 billion  four times less according to forbes data  but as contradictory as it may seem this  situation could have a positive side  many of the big ukrainian oligarchs have  seen a significant proportion of their  productive assets shattered by russia  and with them much of their ability to  influence local communities and national  politics  in other words the destruction of much  of their wealth and influence together  with international assistance and  keeve's change of priorities may lead  these oligarchs to accept changes in the  rules of the game in order to become  entrepreneurs in a competitive market  keep in mind that if keeve really wants  to join the european union it is going  to have to put a stop to all of these  oligarchs let us say that it is very  likely that reconstruction social  pressure and now national security will  be worth much much much more than the  bribes and pressures of these oligarchs  oligarchs who in addition will have to  atone for their past pro-russian  leanings so it would not be strange to  see them suddenly flying the flag of the  european union and reforms  in short in order to become a full  member of the european union ukraine  will have to become a democracy of  certain quality it will have to adopt a  free market economy it will have to  introduce into its law and then apply  the very extensive european legislation  it will have to rebuild its  infrastructure it will have to promote  significant growth rates and end the  power of the oligarchs and completely  change its institutions a very long and  very very complicated to-do list but  wait a minute because it's not just  about ukraine in order to admit such a  large and troubled country as a full  member the european union itself may  have to change i am referring for  example to things like unanimity rules  you see in many areas the european union  requires the unanimity of all member  countries in order to be able to move  forward with various initiatives the  problem is that the european union is  becoming so big that this is becoming  increasingly complicated especially when  we take into account the enormous  differences that exist between the  member countries and to top it all off  ukraine would be a powerful ally of  poland a country that has not been  getting along particularly well with  brussels lately  and keep in mind that this is a risk  that some european leaders are already  talking about openly be that as it may i  think you can see ukraine's entry into  the european union if it ever happens  will be a very long process a very  difficult one and one that is subject to  many conditions but at this point it's  your turn do you think ukraine will  eventually join the european union and  will keeve manage to carry out all the  political and economic reforms necessary  to make ukraine a country that can  compete with eu members leave us your  questions below in the comments and of  course your answers as well and if you  liked this video don't forget to like  and subscribe to our channel so you  don't miss any news once again thank you  so much for being there all the best and  i'll see you next time  [Music]  you
 

